[2026-03-14T21:35:52.813Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dsn8VwkqKXWCjhb3BoCr7t","type":"tool_result","content":"  1000→- **Action**: User clicks on the description text\n  1001→- **Expected**: The description changes to an editable rich text area (supporting markdown) pre-filled with the existing markdown content. The editor is focused and ready for editing.\n  1002→\n  1003→#### Test: Save edited description\n  1004→- **Initial state**: User has clicked the description and it is in edit mode showing \"This is a bug in the login flow\"\n  1005→- **Action**: User changes the text to \"This is a critical bug in the authentication flow that affects all users\" and clicks outside the editor (or presses a save shortcut)\n  1006→- **Expected**: The description updates to show the new rendered markdown. The change is persisted to the database. An activity entry is created recording the description change.\n  1007→\n  1008→#### Test: Description supports markdown formatting\n  1009→- **Initial state**: User has clicked the description and it is in edit mode\n  1010→- **Action**: User types \"## Steps to reproduce\\n1. Go to login\\n2. Enter invalid password\\n3. **Error message** is missing\" and saves\n  1011→- **Expected**: The rendered description shows \"Steps to reproduce\" as a heading, a numbered list with three items, and \"Error message\" in bold.\n  1012→\n  1013→#### Test: Cancel description edit on Escape\n  1014→- **Initial state**: User has clicked the description and it is in edit mode\n  1015→- **Action**: User makes changes to the text and presses Escape\n  1016→- **Expected**: The description reverts to its original content without saving. The editor returns to display mode.\n  1017→\n  1018→#### Test: Edit description multiple times in sequence\n  1019→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page with a description\n  1020→- **Action**: User clicks the description, edits it, saves, then clicks it again to edit a second time\n  1021→- **Expected**: The second edit works correctly — the editor opens with the updated content from the first edit, and subsequent saves persist correctly.\n  1022→\n  1023→### SubIssues\n  1024→\n  1025→#### Test: Sub-issues section renders with heading and add button\n  1026→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page for a parent issue with no sub-issues\n  1027→- **Expected**: The sub-issues section is visible below the description with a heading \"Sub-issues\" and an \"Add sub-issue\" button. When there are no sub-issues, a message like \"No sub-issues\" is displayed.\n  1028→\n  1029→#### Test: Sub-issues list displays child issues\n  1030→- **Initial state**: A parent issue \"ENG-10\" has two sub-issues: \"ENG-11\" (title: \"Design login form\", status: \"Done\") and \"ENG-12\" (title: \"Implement validation\", status: \"In Progress\")\n  1031→- **Expected**: The sub-issues section displays both child issues in a list. Each sub-issue row shows the issue identifier (e.g., \"ENG-11\"), title (clickable), status icon with color, and priority icon. \"ENG-11\" shows a green check-circle (Done) and \"ENG-12\" shows a yellow half-circle (In Progress).\n  1032→\n  1033→#### Test: Click sub-issue navigates to its detail page\n  1034→- **Initial state**: Parent issue \"ENG-10\" has sub-issue \"ENG-11\" displayed in the list\n  1035→- **Action**: User clicks on the title \"Design login form\" of sub-issue \"ENG-11\"\n  1036→- **Expected**: User is navigated to `/issue/ENG-11` (the detail page for the sub-issue). The sub-issue detail page loads showing its full details including a reference to its parent issue.\n  1037→\n  1038→#### Test: Add sub-issue button opens create issue modal\n  1039→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page for issue \"ENG-10\"\n  1040→- **Action**: User clicks the \"Add sub-issue\" button\n  1041→- **Expected**: The create issue modal opens with the parent issue field pre-filled with \"ENG-10\". The team selector defaults to the same team as the parent issue. All other fields are in their default state.\n  1042→\n  1043→#### Test: Newly created sub-issue appears in the list\n  1044→- **Initial state**: User clicked \"Add sub-issue\", the create issue modal is open with parent set to \"ENG-10\"\n  1045→- **Action**: User fills in title \"Write unit tests\", sets status to \"Todo\", and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1046→- **Expected**: The modal closes. The new sub-issue (e.g., \"ENG-13 Write unit tests\") appears in the sub-issues list with a gray circle (Todo) status icon. The sub-issue is persisted with its parent reference.\n  1047→\n  1048→#### Test: Sub-issue status icon updates when status changes\n  1049→- **Initial state**: Sub-issue \"ENG-12\" has status \"In Progress\" (yellow half-circle) and is displayed in the parent's sub-issues list\n  1050→- **Action**: User navigates to \"ENG-12\", changes its status to \"Done\", and navigates back to the parent issue\n  1051→- **Expected**: The sub-issue \"ENG-12\" now shows a green check-circle (Done) icon in the parent's sub-issues list.\n  1052→\n  1053→#### Test: Multiple sub-issues display in correct order\n  1054→- **Initial state**: Parent issue has 4 sub-issues created in order: ENG-11, ENG-12, ENG-13, ENG-14\n  1055→- **Expected**: All 4 sub-issues are displayed in the list in a consistent order (e.g., by creation date or identifier).\n  1056→\n  1057→### ActivityComments\n  1058→\n  1059→#### Test: Activity/Comments section renders with tab toggle\n  1060→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page, scrolled to the bottom of the left panel\n  1061→- **Expected**: The Activity/Comments section is visible with two tab buttons: \"Activity\" and \"Comments\". One tab is active (visually highlighted). A comment input box with a \"Comment\" button is visible at the bottom.\n  1062→\n  1063→#### Test: Activity tab shows chronological history\n  1064→- **Initial state**: An issue has had its status changed from \"Backlog\" to \"Todo\", then assignee changed from unassigned to \"Alice\", then priority changed from \"No Priority\" to \"High\"\n  1065→- **Action**: User clicks the \"Activity\" tab\n  1066→- **Expected**: The activity tab displays three chronological entries: (1) status change from Backlog to Todo with timestamp and actor name, (2) assignee change to Alice with timestamp and actor name, (3) priority change to High with timestamp and actor name. Each entry shows the actor's avatar, their name, a description of the change, and a relative timestamp. Entries are ordered oldest first (top) to newest (bottom).\n  1067→\n  1068→#### Test: Comments tab shows user comments\n  1069→- **Initial state**: An issue has two comments: one by \"Alice\" saying \"I'll look into this\" posted 2 hours ago, and one by \"Bob\" saying \"Found the root cause\" posted 1 hour ago\n  1070→- **Action**: User clicks the \"Comments\" tab\n  1071→- **Expected**: Two comments are displayed, each showing the author's avatar, name, relative timestamp (e.g., \"2h ago\", \"1h ago\"), and the comment content. Comments are ordered oldest first.\n  1072→\n  1073→#### Test: Add a new comment\n  1074→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page with the comments section visible. The comment input box is empty.\n  1075→- **Action**: User types \"This should be fixed in the next sprint\" in the comment input and clicks the \"Comment\" button\n  1076→- **Expected**: The new comment appears at the bottom of the comments list showing the current user's avatar, name, \"just now\" as the timestamp, and the comment text \"This should be fixed in the next sprint\". The comment input is cleared. The comment is persisted to the database. A notification is created for subscribers of this issue.\n  1077→\n  1078→#### Test: Comment button is disabled when input is empty\n  1079→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page, the comment input box is empty\n  1080→- **Expected**: The \"Comment\" button is disabled (grayed out, not clickable).\n  1081→\n  1082→#### Test: Comment button enables when text is entered\n  1083→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page, the comment input box is empty and the button is disabled\n  1084→- **Action**: User types \"Test comment\" in the comment input\n  1085→- **Expected**: The \"Comment\" button becomes enabled (visually active, clickable).\n  1086→\n  1087→#### Test: Submit comment via Enter key\n  1088→- **Initial state**: User has typed \"Quick fix needed\" in the comment input\n  1089→- **Action**: User presses Enter (or Ctrl+Enter / Cmd+Enter depending on implementation)\n  1090→- **Expected**: The comment is submitted and appears in the comments list. The input is cleared.\n  1091→\n  1092→#### Test: Switch between Activity and Comments tabs\n  1093→- **Initial state**: User is on the \"Comments\" tab viewing comments\n  1094→- **Action**: User clicks the \"Activity\" tab\n  1095→- **Expected**: The view switches to show activity history entries. The \"Activity\" tab is now visually highlighted and \"Comments\" tab is not. Clicking \"Comments\" again switches back to the comments view with all comments still visible.\n  1096→\n  1097→#### Test: Activity tab updates when issue properties change\n  1098→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page with the \"Activity\" tab selected, showing 2 existing activity entries\n  1099→- **Action**: User changes the issue status from \"Todo\" to \"In Progress\" via the status selector\n  1100→- **Expected**: A new activity entry appears at the bottom of the activity list showing \"changed status from Todo to In Progress\" with the current user's name, avatar, and \"just now\" timestamp. The total count is now 3 entries.\n  1101→\n  1102→#### Test: Add multiple comments in sequence\n  1103→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page with the Comments tab active\n  1104→- **Action**: User types \"First comment\" and clicks \"Comment\", then types \"Second comment\" and clicks \"Comment\"\n  1105→- **Expected**: Both comments appear in the list in order. The input clears after each submission. Both comments are persisted correctly.\n  1106→\n  1107→#### Test: Comment displays long text properly\n  1108→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page\n  1109→- **Action**: User types a long comment (multiple paragraphs) and submits it\n  1110→- **Expected**: The comment is displayed with proper text wrapping and paragraph spacing. No content is truncated or overflows the container.\n  1111→\n  1112→### IssueSidebar\n  1113→\n  1114→#### Test: Sidebar renders all property fields\n  1115→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page for an issue with status \"In Progress\", priority \"High\", assignee \"Alice\", labels [\"Bug\", \"Frontend\"], project \"Auth Rewrite\", cycle \"Sprint 3\", due date \"2026-04-01\"\n  1116→- **Expected**: The right sidebar displays all property fields: Status showing \"In Progress\" with half-circle yellow icon, Priority showing \"High\" with orange arrow-up icon, Assignee showing \"Alice\" with avatar, Labels showing \"Bug\" and \"Frontend\" as colored badges, Project showing \"Auth Rewrite\", Cycle showing \"Sprint 3\", Due Date showing \"Apr 1, 2026\", Created timestamp (read-only), and Updated timestamp (read-only).\n  1117→\n  1118→#### Test: Change status via sidebar dropdown\n  1119→- **Initial state**: Issue has status \"Todo\". User is on the issue detail page.\n  1120→- **Action**: User clicks the Status field in the sidebar\n  1121→- **Expected**: A dropdown appears listing all statuses (Backlog, Todo, In Progress, In Review, Done, Cancelled) with their respective icons and colors.\n  1122→\n  1123→#### Test: Select new status in sidebar\n  1124→- **Initial state**: The status dropdown is open in the sidebar, current status is \"Todo\"\n  1125→- **Action**: User clicks \"Done\"\n  1126→- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Status updates to \"Done\" with a green check-circle icon. The change is persisted. An activity entry is created. The inline status selector in the header also updates to \"Done\".\n  1127→\n  1128→#### Test: Change priority via sidebar dropdown\n  1129→- **Initial state**: Issue has priority \"Medium\". User is on the issue detail page.\n  1130→- **Action**: User clicks the Priority field in the sidebar\n  1131→- **Expected**: A dropdown appears listing all priorities (Urgent, High, Medium, Low, No Priority) with their respective icons and colors.\n  1132→\n  1133→#### Test: Select new priority in sidebar\n  1134→- **Initial state**: The priority dropdown is open, current priority is \"Medium\"\n  1135→- **Action**: User clicks \"Urgent\"\n  1136→- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Priority updates to \"Urgent\" with a red alert-triangle icon. The change is persisted. An activity entry is created recording the priority change from \"Medium\" to \"Urgent\".\n  1137→\n  1138→#### Test: Change assignee via searchable selector\n  1139→- **Initial state**: Issue is unassigned. The workspace has members \"Alice\", \"Bob\", and \"Charlie\".\n  1140→- **Action**: User clicks the Assignee field in the sidebar\n  1141→- **Expected**: A searchable dropdown appears showing all workspace members with their avatars and names.\n  1142→\n  1143→#### Test: Search and select assignee\n  1144→- **Initial state**: The assignee dropdown is open showing all members\n  1145→- **Action**: User types \"Ali\" in the search field\n  1146→- **Expected**: The list filters to show only \"Alice\". User clicks \"Alice\".\n  1147→- **Action**: User clicks \"Alice\"\n  1148→- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Assignee updates to show Alice's avatar and name. The change is persisted. An activity entry is created. A notification is sent to Alice that she was assigned to this issue.\n  1149→\n  1150→#### Test: Remove assignee\n  1151→- **Initial state**: Issue is assigned to \"Alice\"\n  1152→- **Action**: User clicks the Assignee field, then clicks an \"Unassign\" or clear option\n  1153→- **Expected**: The assignee is removed. The field shows as unassigned (e.g., \"No assignee\" or empty avatar placeholder). The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n  1154→\n  1155→#### Test: Add labels via multi-select picker\n  1156→- **Initial state**: Issue has no labels. Available labels include \"Bug\" (red), \"Feature\" (green), \"Improvement\" (blue).\n  1157→- **Action**: User clicks the Labels field in the sidebar\n  1158→- **Expected**: A multi-select dropdown appears showing all available labels with their colored dots and names. No labels are checked.\n  1159→\n  1160→#### Test: Select multiple labels\n  1161→- **Initial state**: The labels picker is open, no labels selected\n  1162→- **Action**: User clicks \"Bug\", then clicks \"Frontend\"\n  1163→- **Expected**: Both \"Bug\" and \"Frontend\" are checked in the dropdown. The sidebar field updates to show both labels as colored badges. The changes are persisted. Activity entries are created for each label addition.\n  1164→\n  1165→#### Test: Remove a label\n  1166→- **Initial state**: Issue has labels \"Bug\" and \"Frontend\". The labels picker is open.\n  1167→- **Action**: User unchecks \"Bug\"\n  1168→- **Expected**: \"Bug\" is removed from the issue. Only \"Frontend\" badge remains in the sidebar. The change is persisted. An activity entry is created recording the label removal.\n  1169→\n  1170→#### Test: Change project via searchable selector\n  1171→- **Initial state**: Issue has no project assigned. Available projects include \"Auth Rewrite\", \"Performance Optimization\", \"Mobile App\".\n  1172→- **Action**: User clicks the Project field in the sidebar\n  1173→- **Expected**: A searchable dropdown appears listing available projects.\n  1174→\n  1175→#### Test: Search and select project\n  1176→- **Initial state**: The project dropdown is open\n  1177→- **Action**: User types \"Auth\" in the search field, then clicks \"Auth Rewrite\"\n  1178→- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Project updates to show \"Auth Rewrite\". The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n  1179→\n  1180→#### Test: Remove project assignment\n  1181→- **Initial state**: Issue is assigned to project \"Auth Rewrite\"\n  1182→- **Action**: User clicks the Project field, then clicks a \"Remove\" or clear option\n  1183→- **Expected**: The project is unassigned. The field shows as empty (e.g., \"No project\"). The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n  1184→\n  1185→#### Test: Change cycle via dropdown\n  1186→- **Initial state**: Issue belongs to team \"Engineering\" which has cycles \"Sprint 2\" (completed), \"Sprint 3\" (active), \"Sprint 4\" (upcoming). Issue has no cycle assigned.\n  1187→- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle field in the sidebar\n  1188→- **Expected**: A dropdown appears showing available cycles for the team: \"Sprint 2\", \"Sprint 3\", \"Sprint 4\" with their date ranges.\n  1189→\n  1190→#### Test: Select cycle\n  1191→- **Initial state**: The cycle dropdown is open\n  1192→- **Action**: User clicks \"Sprint 3\"\n  1193→- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Cycle updates to show \"Sprint 3\". The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n  1194→\n  1195→#### Test: Change due date via date picker\n  1196→- **Initial state**: Issue has no due date set\n  1197→- **Action**: User clicks the Due Date field in the sidebar\n  1198→- **Expected**: A date picker opens allowing the user to select a date.\n  1199→\n  1200→#### Test: Select due date\n  1201→- **Initial state**: The date picker is open\n  1202→- **Action**: User selects April 15, 2026\n  1203→- **Expected**: The date picker closes. Due Date updates to show \"Apr 15, 2026\". The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n  1204→\n  1205→#### Test: Clear due date\n  1206→- **Initial state**: Issue has due date \"Apr 15, 2026\"\n  1207→- **Action**: User clicks the Due Date field and clicks a \"Clear\" or remove option\n  1208→- **Expected**: The due date is removed. The field shows as empty (e.g., \"No due date\"). The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n  1209→\n  1210→#### Test: Created and Updated timestamps are read-only\n  1211→- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page. The issue was created on \"Mar 10, 2026\" and last updated \"Mar 14, 2026\".\n  1212→- **Expected**: The Created field shows \"Mar 10, 2026\" (or relative time like \"4 days ago\") and the Updated field shows \"Mar 14, 2026\" (or \"today\"). Both fields are not clickable/editable — they have no hover cursor change or click handler.\n  1213→\n  1214→#### Test: Sidebar dropdowns close on outside click\n  1215→- **Initial state**: The priority dropdown is open in the sidebar\n  1216→- **Action**: User clicks outside the dropdown (e.g., on the description area)\n  1217→- **Expected**: The dropdown closes without changing the priority value.\n  1218→\n  1219→#### Test: Use assignee selector multiple times in sequence\n  1220→- **Initial state**: Issue is assigned to \"Alice\"\n  1221→- **Action**: User clicks the Assignee field, selects \"Bob\", then immediately clicks the Assignee field again and selects \"Charlie\"\n  1222→- **Expected**: The assignee correctly updates to \"Charlie\" after the second selection. Both changes create separate activity entries. The search input resets between uses.\n  1223→\n  1224→## Create Issue Modal\n  1225→\n  1226→**Components**: CreateIssueForm, CreateIssueActions\n  1227→\n  1228→### CreateIssueForm\n  1229→\n  1230→#### Test: Create issue modal renders with all form fields\n  1231→- **Initial state**: User is on Team Issues page for team \"Engineering\"\n  1232→- **Expected**: Modal displays: Team selector dropdown, Title text input, Description text area, Status dropdown, Priority dropdown, Assignee searchable selector, Labels multi-select, Project searchable selector, Cycle dropdown, Due date picker, and Parent issue searchable selector. All fields are visible and properly labeled.\n  1233→\n  1234→#### Test: Modal opens from New Issue button on team issues page\n  1235→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/:teamId/issues` for team \"Engineering\"\n  1236→- **Action**: User clicks the \"New Issue\" button\n  1237→- **Expected**: The create issue modal opens as an overlay. The Team selector is pre-selected to \"Engineering\". The Title input is focused.\n  1238→\n  1239→#### Test: Modal opens from keyboard shortcut C\n  1240→- **Initial state**: User is on `/my-issues`, no input fields are focused\n  1241→- **Action**: User presses \"C\" key\n  1242→- **Expected**: The create issue modal opens as an overlay.\n  1243→\n  1244→#### Test: Team selector defaults to current team context\n  1245→- **Initial state**: User opens create issue modal from team \"Design\" issues page\n  1246→- **Expected**: The Team selector dropdown shows \"Design\" as the selected value.\n  1247→\n  1248→#### Test: Team selector allows changing team\n  1249→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" pre-selected. Teams \"Engineering\" and \"Design\" exist.\n  1250→- **Action**: User clicks the Team selector dropdown and selects \"Design\"\n  1251→- **Expected**: Team selector now shows \"Design\". The Cycle dropdown updates to show \"Design\" team's cycles instead of \"Engineering\" team's cycles.\n  1252→\n  1253→#### Test: Title field is required\n  1254→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1255→- **Action**: User leaves the Title field empty and attempts to submit\n  1256→- **Expected**: A validation error appears indicating that title is required. The issue is not created.\n  1257→\n  1258→#### Test: Title field accepts text input\n  1259→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1260→- **Action**: User types \"Implement user authentication\" in the Title field\n  1261→- **Expected**: The Title field displays \"Implement user authentication\".\n  1262→\n  1263→#### Test: Description field supports markdown text entry\n  1264→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1265→- **Action**: User types \"## Overview\\nThis issue covers the auth flow\" in the Description text area\n  1266→- **Expected**: The Description field accepts and displays the markdown text.\n  1267→\n  1268→#### Test: Status dropdown defaults to Backlog\n  1269→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1270→- **Expected**: The Status dropdown shows \"Backlog\" with a dotted circle icon in gray as the default selected value.\n  1271→\n  1272→#### Test: Status dropdown shows all status options with icons\n  1273→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1274→- **Action**: User clicks the Status dropdown\n  1275→- **Expected**: Dropdown shows all statuses: Backlog (dotted circle, gray), Todo (circle, gray), In Progress (half circle, yellow), In Review (three-quarter circle, blue), Done (check circle, green), Cancelled (x-circle, red).\n  1276→\n  1277→#### Test: Status dropdown allows selecting a different status\n  1278→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Status shows \"Backlog\"\n  1279→- **Action**: User clicks Status dropdown and selects \"Todo\"\n  1280→- **Expected**: Status dropdown now shows \"Todo\" with circle icon in gray.\n  1281→\n  1282→#### Test: Priority dropdown defaults to No Priority\n  1283→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1284→- **Expected**: The Priority dropdown shows \"No Priority\" with dots-horizontal icon in gray as the default value.\n  1285→\n  1286→#### Test: Priority dropdown shows all priority options with icons\n  1287→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1288→- **Action**: User clicks the Priority dropdown\n  1289→- **Expected**: Dropdown shows: Urgent (alert-triangle, red), High (arrow-up, orange), Medium (minus, yellow), Low (arrow-down, blue), No Priority (dots-horizontal, gray).\n  1290→\n  1291→#### Test: Priority dropdown allows selecting a priority\n  1292→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Priority shows \"No Priority\"\n  1293→- **Action**: User clicks Priority dropdown and selects \"High\"\n  1294→- **Expected**: Priority dropdown now shows \"High\" with arrow-up icon in orange.\n  1295→\n  1296→#### Test: Assignee selector is searchable\n  1297→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. Workspace has members \"Alice Smith\", \"Bob Jones\", \"Carol Lee\".\n  1298→- **Action**: User clicks the Assignee selector and types \"Ali\"\n  1299→- **Expected**: The selector filters to show only \"Alice Smith\" with her avatar.\n  1300→\n  1301→#### Test: Assignee selector allows selecting a member\n  1302→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Assignee field is empty\n  1303→- **Action**: User clicks the Assignee selector, types \"Bob\", and clicks \"Bob Jones\"\n  1304→- **Expected**: Assignee field shows \"Bob Jones\" with his avatar.\n  1305→\n  1306→#### Test: Labels multi-select allows selecting multiple labels\n  1307→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. Labels \"Bug\", \"Feature\", \"Improvement\" exist.\n  1308→- **Action**: User clicks Labels selector, selects \"Bug\", then selects \"Feature\"\n  1309→- **Expected**: Both \"Bug\" and \"Feature\" appear as colored badges in the Labels field.\n  1310→\n  1311→#### Test: Labels multi-select allows removing a selected label\n  1312→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with \"Bug\" and \"Feature\" labels selected\n  1313→- **Action**: User clicks the remove button on the \"Bug\" label badge\n  1314→- **Expected**: Only \"Feature\" remains selected in the Labels field.\n  1315→\n  1316→#### Test: Project selector is searchable\n  1317→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. Projects \"API Redesign\", \"Mobile App\", \"Dashboard\" exist.\n  1318→- **Action**: User clicks the Project selector and types \"API\"\n  1319→- **Expected**: The selector filters to show only \"API Redesign\".\n  1320→\n  1321→#### Test: Project selector allows selecting a project\n  1322→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Project field is empty\n  1323→- **Action**: User clicks the Project selector and selects \"Mobile App\"\n  1324→- **Expected**: Project field shows \"Mobile App\".\n  1325→\n  1326→#### Test: Cycle dropdown shows team's available cycles\n  1327→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected. Team has cycles \"Sprint 10\" (active) and \"Sprint 11\" (upcoming).\n  1328→- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle dropdown\n  1329→- **Expected**: Dropdown shows \"Sprint 10\" and \"Sprint 11\". The active cycle is indicated.\n  1330→\n  1331→#### Test: Cycle dropdown allows selecting a cycle\n  1332→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Cycle field is empty\n  1333→- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle dropdown and selects \"Sprint 10\"\n  1334→- **Expected**: Cycle field shows \"Sprint 10\".\n  1335→\n  1336→#### Test: Cycle dropdown updates when team is changed\n  1337→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected showing its cycles\n  1338→- **Action**: User changes team to \"Design\" via the Team selector\n  1339→- **Expected**: The Cycle dropdown clears its selection and now shows \"Design\" team's cycles instead of \"Engineering\" cycles.\n  1340→\n  1341→#### Test: Due date picker allows selecting a date\n  1342→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Due date field is empty\n  1343→- **Action**: User clicks the Due date picker and selects a date (e.g., March 25, 2026)\n  1344→- **Expected**: Due date field shows \"Mar 25, 2026\".\n  1345→\n  1346→#### Test: Due date picker allows clearing the date\n  1347→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with due date \"Mar 25, 2026\" set\n  1348→- **Action**: User clicks the clear button on the Due date field\n  1349→- **Expected**: Due date field is empty again.\n  1350→\n  1351→#### Test: Parent issue selector is searchable\n  1352→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. Issues \"ENG-1: Setup project\", \"ENG-2: Design database\" exist.\n  1353→- **Action**: User clicks the Parent issue selector and types \"Setup\"\n  1354→- **Expected**: The selector filters to show \"ENG-1: Setup project\".\n  1355→\n  1356→#### Test: Parent issue selector allows selecting a parent\n  1357→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Parent issue field is empty\n  1358→- **Action**: User clicks the Parent issue selector and selects \"ENG-1: Setup project\"\n  1359→- **Expected**: Parent issue field shows \"ENG-1: Setup project\".\n  1360→\n  1361→#### Test: Assignee selector can be used multiple times\n  1362→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with \"Alice Smith\" selected as assignee\n  1363→- **Action**: User clicks the Assignee selector, clears the selection, types \"Bob\", selects \"Bob Jones\", then repeats to select \"Carol Lee\"\n  1364→- **Expected**: Assignee field shows \"Carol Lee\" after the final selection. Each selection correctly replaces the previous one.\n  1365→\n  1366→#### Test: Labels selector can be used multiple times in sequence\n  1367→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with no labels selected\n  1368→- **Action**: User selects \"Bug\" label, then opens the selector again and adds \"Feature\", then opens again and removes \"Bug\" and adds \"Improvement\"\n  1369→- **Expected**: Labels field shows \"Feature\" and \"Improvement\" as colored badges.\n  1370→\n  1371→#### Test: Status dropdown can be changed multiple times\n  1372→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with Status \"Backlog\"\n  1373→- **Action**: User changes status to \"Todo\", then changes it to \"In Progress\", then back to \"Backlog\"\n  1374→- **Expected**: Status shows \"Backlog\" after the final change. Each intermediate state was correctly displayed.\n  1375→\n  1376→### CreateIssueActions\n  1377→\n  1378→#### Test: Create Issue button is visible and styled\n  1379→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1380→- **Expected**: A \"Create Issue\" button is visible at the bottom of the modal with primary styling (accent color background).\n  1381→\n  1382→#### Test: Cancel button is visible\n  1383→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1384→- **Expected**: A \"Cancel\" button is visible at the bottom of the modal alongside the \"Create Issue\" button.\n  1385→\n  1386→#### Test: Cancel button closes the modal\n  1387→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1388→- **Action**: User clicks the \"Cancel\" button\n  1389→- **Expected**: The modal closes. No issue is created. The user returns to the previous page view.\n  1390→\n  1391→#### Test: Cancel button discards form data\n  1392→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. User has entered title \"Draft issue\" and selected priority \"High\".\n  1393→- **Action**: User clicks \"Cancel\", then opens the create issue modal again\n  1394→- **Expected**: The modal opens with all fields reset to defaults (empty title, Backlog status, No Priority). The previously entered data is not retained.\n  1395→\n  1396→#### Test: Create Issue button submits the form with all fields\n  1397→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. User has filled in: Title \"Implement OAuth\", Description \"Add OAuth support\", Status \"Todo\", Priority \"High\", Assignee \"Alice Smith\", Labels \"Feature\", Project \"API Redesign\", Cycle \"Sprint 10\", Due date \"Mar 25, 2026\".\n  1398→- **Action**: User clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1399→- **Expected**: The issue is created with all specified field values. A brief confirmation showing the new issue identifier (e.g., \"ENG-43\") is displayed. The modal closes and the user is navigated to the issues list.\n  1400→\n  1401→#### Test: Create Issue with only required fields\n  1402→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected\n  1403→- **Action**: User enters title \"Quick bug fix\" and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1404→- **Expected**: The issue is created with title \"Quick bug fix\", status \"Backlog\", priority \"No Priority\", and no assignee/labels/project/cycle/due date. The new issue identifier is briefly shown. The modal closes.\n  1405→\n  1406→#### Test: Created issue appears in the team issues list\n  1407→- **Initial state**: User is on Team Issues page for \"Engineering\". The page shows existing issues.\n  1408→- **Action**: User clicks \"New Issue\", enters title \"New feature request\", sets status to \"Todo\", and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1409→- **Expected**: After modal closes, the Team Issues list updates to include \"New feature request\" under the \"Todo\" status group with the correct issue identifier (e.g., \"ENG-44\").\n  1410→\n  1411→#### Test: Created issue with parent appears as sub-issue\n  1412→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. Issue \"ENG-1: Setup project\" exists.\n  1413→- **Action**: User enters title \"Write unit tests\", selects \"ENG-1: Setup project\" as parent issue, and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1414→- **Expected**: The issue is created. When navigating to \"ENG-1: Setup project\" detail page, \"Write unit tests\" appears in the sub-issues section.\n  1415→\n  1416→#### Test: Created issue generates activity history entry\n  1417→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1418→- **Action**: User creates an issue with title \"Track history\" and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1419→- **Expected**: When viewing the new issue's detail page, the Activity tab shows an initial \"created this issue\" entry with the current user's name and timestamp.\n  1420→\n  1421→#### Test: Created issue generates inbox notification for assignee\n  1422→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. Current user is \"Alice Smith\".\n  1423→- **Action**: User creates an issue with title \"Review PR\", assigns it to \"Bob Jones\", and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1424→- **Expected**: When Bob Jones checks their inbox, a notification appears: \"Alice Smith assigned you to [issue identifier]: Review PR\" with the correct timestamp. The inbox badge count increases.\n  1425→\n  1426→#### Test: Create Issue button is disabled during submission\n  1427→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with valid data entered\n  1428→- **Action**: User clicks \"Create Issue\"\n  1429→- **Expected**: The \"Create Issue\" button becomes disabled (to prevent duplicate submissions) while the request is processing. It re-enables after the operation completes (success or failure).\n  1430→\n  1431→#### Test: Clicking outside the modal closes it\n  1432→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1433→- **Action**: User clicks on the overlay area outside the modal\n  1434→- **Expected**: The modal closes without creating an issue.\n  1435→\n  1436→#### Test: Pressing Escape closes the modal\n  1437→- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open\n  1438→- **Action**: User presses the Escape key\n  1439→- **Expected**: The modal closes without creating an issue.\n  1440→\n  1441→#### Test: Creating multiple issues in sequence works correctly\n  1442→- **Initial state**: User is on Team Issues page for \"Engineering\"\n  1443→- **Action**: User creates issue \"First task\" via the modal, then opens the modal again and creates \"Second task\"\n  1444→- **Expected**: Both issues appear in the team issues list with sequential identifiers (e.g., \"ENG-43\", \"ENG-44\"). The modal resets correctly between creations.\n  1445→\n  1446→## Active Cycle Page (`/team/:teamId/cycles`)\n  1447→\n  1448→**Components**: CycleList, CycleDetail, CreateCycle, BurndownChart\n  1449→\n  1450→### CycleList\n  1451→\n  1452→#### Test: Cycle list renders all team cycles with name, date range, progress bar, and issue count\n  1453→- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/eng-1/cycles`. The \"Engineering\" team has 3 cycles: \"Sprint 1\" (Jan 1–Jan 14, 8/10 issues done), \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28, 3/8 issues done, active), \"Sprint 3\" (Jan 29–Feb 11, 0/5 issues done).\n  1454→- **Expected**: A list of 3 cycle rows is displayed. Each row shows the cycle name, date range (e.g., \"Jan 1 – Jan 14\"), a progress bar reflecting the completion percentage (e.g., 80% for Sprint 1), and the issue count (e.g., \"10 issues\"). Rows are ordered chronologically.\n  1455→\n  1456→#### Test: Active cycle is visually highlighted in the list\n  1457→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`. \"Sprint 2\" is the active cycle.\n  1458→- **Expected**: The \"Sprint 2\" row has a distinct visual highlight (e.g., accent border, background color, or \"Active\" badge) that distinguishes it from the other cycles. Non-active cycles do not have this highlight.\n  1459→\n  1460→#### Test: Clicking a cycle navigates to cycle detail view\n  1461→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` viewing the cycle list.\n  1462→- **Action**: User clicks on the \"Sprint 2\" cycle row.\n  1463→- **Expected**: The cycle detail view for \"Sprint 2\" is displayed, showing the cycle name, date range, progress stats, issue list, and burndown chart.\n  1464→\n  1465→#### Test: Cycle list shows empty state when team has no cycles\n  1466→- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/design-1/cycles`. The \"Design\" team has no cycles.\n  1467→- **Expected**: An empty state message is displayed (e.g., \"No cycles yet. Create your first cycle to get started.\") with an icon. The \"New Cycle\" button is still visible.\n  1468→\n  1469→#### Test: Cycle list progress bar accurately reflects issue completion percentage\n  1470→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`. \"Sprint 1\" has 8 of 10 issues done (80%), \"Sprint 2\" has 3 of 8 done (37.5%), \"Sprint 3\" has 0 of 5 done (0%).\n  1471→- **Expected**: Each cycle's progress bar width corresponds to its completion percentage. Sprint 1 shows ~80% filled, Sprint 2 shows ~37.5% filled, Sprint 3 shows 0% filled (empty bar).\n  1472→\n  1473→#### Test: Cycle list updates when a new cycle is created\n  1474→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` with 2 existing cycles.\n  1475→- **Action**: User creates a new cycle \"Sprint 4\" via the \"New Cycle\" button.\n  1476→- **Expected**: The cycle list now shows 3 cycles, with \"Sprint 4\" appearing in the list with 0 issues and an empty progress bar.\n  1477→\n  1478→#### Test: Clicking a different cycle after viewing one switches the detail view\n  1479→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` and has clicked \"Sprint 2\" to view its detail.\n  1480→- **Action**: User clicks back to the cycle list and clicks \"Sprint 1\".\n  1481→- **Expected**: The detail view updates to show \"Sprint 1\" cycle information (name, date range, stats, issues, burndown chart). No stale data from \"Sprint 2\" is shown.\n  1482→\n  1483→### CycleDetail\n  1484→\n  1485→#### Test: Cycle detail view renders cycle name and date range\n  1486→- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` and clicks on \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28).\n  1487→- **Expected**: The detail view header displays the cycle name \"Sprint 2\" and the date range \"Jan 15 – Jan 28\" prominently at the top.\n  1488→\n  1489→#### Test: Cycle detail shows progress stats (total, completed, in progress, remaining)\n  1490→- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" which has 8 total issues: 3 done, 2 in progress, 3 remaining (backlog/todo).\n  1491→- **Expected**: Four progress stat cards/sections are displayed: \"Total: 8\", \"Completed: 3\", \"In Progress: 2\", \"Remaining: 3\". The stats are clearly labeled and visually distinct.\n  1492→\n  1493→#### Test: Cycle detail shows progress bar with correct completion percentage\n  1494→- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 3 of 8 issues completed (37.5%).\n  1495→- **Expected**: A progress bar is displayed showing approximately 37.5% completion. The percentage text \"37.5%\" (or \"3 of 8\") is visible near the progress bar.\n  1496→\n  1497→#### Test: Cycle detail displays filtered issue list matching Team Issues layout\n  1498→- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" which has 8 issues across various statuses and priorities.\n  1499→- **Expected**: An issue list is displayed below the progress stats using the same layout as the Team Issues page. Each issue row shows: priority icon, issue identifier (e.g., \"ENG-42\"), title (clickable), status icon, labels as colored badges, assignee avatar, due date, and project name. Issues are grouped by status by default."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VZCcEgJMeh3Ga6cQedHgo4","session_id":"dd761dfc-3c41-4aea-8240-b38c3f09e95a","uuid":"d4152378-2512-4047-ad46-8063d43a1150"}
